A||||9||FieroPerformance||Will Isuzu 5 speed take a stock V6 clutch and pressure plate?||||1||
Z||000000||FieroPerformance||12-03-2001||10:20 PM||||Alright. I had a V6 clutch and pressure plate but I took it back because I heard that the inside of the transmission bell housing needed to be modified for the V6 plate. The clutch that I put in, althought it is supposed to be heavy duty, is slipping. Plus I am now hearing from people that say there is no modification needed to put a stock V6 clutch setup on the 5 speed Isuzu. Someone who has done this conversion themself (aka not paid to have it done) or seen it done through every step, please let me know the truth.<P>I am not going to pay $250+ for a centerforce clutch. I am either going to put the stock (or heavy duty) V6 clutch in or buy a V6 4 speed tranny and put in a V6 clutch.<P>Thanks.<BR>-Charlie<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/newsigfperformance.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by FieroPerformance (edited 12-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||12-04-2001||12:07 AM||||I've always used the clutch and pressure plate that is called for the tranny in use. (unless it is inadequate for the engine-custom) Others may not line up with the splines on the input shaft of the trans.||||reg||1
Z||000002||SCCA FIERO||12-04-2001||01:23 AM||||I learned this the hard way. NO, it will not! The car just wouldn't move with the v6 components in there. <P>------------------<BR>-Chris <BR>88 Fiero Coupe (V6 & fastback conversion)<BR><B>You must be fast, cuz I was haulin ass when I passed you!</B>||||reg||1
Z||000003||jimmybpei||12-04-2001||12:58 PM||||Tahts funny you guys say that it won't work because my caddy has a 5 spd isuzu with the v6 clutch palte R-bearing and flywheele. <P>It seem s to move rite along with no grinding at all. I installed everything myself.<P>Later<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jimmybpeisig_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroPerformance||12-04-2001||02:45 PM||||The clutch that matches this tranny is not adequate. It is not holding at WOT above 4,000 RPM. I am running a stock 2.8 (freshly rebuilt) with a performance cam, high compression power forged pistons, exhaust valves ported and flanges drilled out of the exhaust manifolds.<P>-Charlie||||reg||1
Z||000005||FieroMaster88||12-04-2001||03:54 PM||||Well...I have the same cobo in my car.  V6 (bored out to a 3.1) and an isuzu 5 speed.  My problems have been with hydrolics, but other than that my AutoZone clutch grips like a mofo.  Never slips.  If I mash the gas of the line and let the clutch out it grabs.....tires spin before the clutch slips!  I love it.  I would say use the isuzu clutch with the Isuzu trans!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jamessig.jpg">                   <BR>James Essar<BR>AOL IM Name: FieroMaster88<BR>Not your ordinary 88 coupe!||||reg||1
Z||000006||SCCA FIERO||12-04-2001||04:53 PM||||My 2.8 has the Isuzu 5 speed with the V6 flywheel, Isuzu TO bearing, Isuzu pressure plate and the Isuzu clutch. I installed these myself after the V6 clutch, plate, flywheel and Isuzu TO bearing wouldn't even get the car going. The Isuzu TO bearing couldn't match up with the fingers on the V6 plate.<P>If yours works, I have no clue why it does, or why mine didn't. All I know is I had to drop the cradle and replace it all over again. It works like a charm with no slipping (AutoZone clutch as well).||||reg||1
Z||000007||tesmith66||12-04-2001||05:03 PM||||I'm running the Isuzu 5 speed. I have the Getrag clutch and pressure plate and the Isuzu T/O bearing behind a fresh 2.8 with the Crane cam, flat top pistons and ported manifolds.<P>All AutoZone parts and no problems. Grabs like a 3 year old in a toy store!<P>------------------<BR>Timothy E. Smith<BR>1986 SE V6<BR>1984 Coupe<p>[This message has been edited by tesmith66 (edited 12-04-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000008||jelly2m8||12-04-2001||08:03 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by tesmith66:<BR><B>I'm running the Isuzu 5 speed. I have the Getrag clutch and pressure plate and the Isuzu T/O bearing behind a fresh 2.8 with the Crane cam, flat top pistons and ported manifolds.<P>All AutoZone parts and no problems. Grabs like a 3 year old in a toy store!<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P> The V6 4 spd clutch doesn't clear in the Isuzu bellhousing. I haven't tried the Getrag, so it sounds like this is the solution.<P> This must be what jimmyb is using. The 4 spd release bearing is too big to work with the Isuzu release bearing fork, and flops around on the input shaft sleeve.<P> Neat, you learn something new everyday!<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||FieroPerformance||12-04-2001||10:20 PM||||Yeah, the people that have said they got it to work say that it was the 5 speed V6 (getrag) clutch setup. They also say they use the Isuzu T/O bearing. I am going to try it. I just got done fixing a CV boot clamp and took my car for a spin. The clutch is getting worse, but still only slipping durring WOT in the upper RPM range.<P>Thanks for the help everyone.<P>-Charlie||||reg||1
